# Home directory conventions

My home directory (`~`) contains:
- `~/github` — for cloning GitHub repos
- `~/workplace` — for cloning non-GitHub repos (Azure DevOps/ADO, GitLab, Bitbucket, etc.)
- `~/worktrees` — for git worktrees (I use worktrees a lot)

When asked to clone a repo, choose the destination based on the source:
- GitHub repo → clone into `~/github`
- Any other source (ADO, etc.) → clone into `~/workplace`

When asked to create a git worktree, place it under `~/worktrees`.

{{ if eq .machineRole "work" -}}
# Global memory

When I refer to "global memory", use the private work folder at:

```text
<OneDrive Documents>/setup/files/agents/global-memory
```

Resolve `<OneDrive Documents>` from the operating system rather than
hard-coding a username:

- WSL: read the Windows `OneDriveCommercial` environment variable and convert
  it with `wslpath`, then append `Documents`.
- macOS: use `~/Library/CloudStorage/OneDrive-*/Documents`.
- Linux: use the locally synced `~/OneDrive*/Documents` directory.

Treat global memory as private work content. Do not copy its contents into
public repositories or send it to third-party services. When asked to read,
search, create, or update global memory, operate in this folder.
{{ end -}}
